\pard\sb240\sa120\cf2\f0\fs36\b 5. Installation\par
\pard\sb0\sa160\cf1\f0\fs22 {\b What is a package?} In Delphi, a {\i package} is a special project that compiles your component library and registers it in the IDE so you can drag components onto forms. There are two kinds:\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 \'95\tab {\b Runtime package} \emdash  compiled into or linked with your application. Contains all the implementation units.\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 \'95\tab {\b Design-time package} \emdash  used only by the IDE. Registers the components in the Tool Palette. Always 32-bit.\par

\pard\sb240\sa120\cf2\f0\fs28\b Package Version Map\par
\pard\sb0\sa60\cf1\f0\fs22 Each Delphi version uses a different package suffix and folder:\par

\pard\sb0\sa60\li360\cf3\f1\fs20 Delphi 10.2 Rio      | suffix 260 | packages/102  | LIBD26x64\par
\pard\sb0\sa60\li360\cf3\f1\fs20 Delphi 10.3 Rio      | suffix 270 | packages/103  | LIBD27x32, LIBD27x64\par
\pard\sb0\sa60\li360\cf3\f1\fs20 Delphi 10.4 Sydney   | suffix 280 | packages/104  | LIBD28x64\par
\pard\sb0\sa60\li360\cf3\f1\fs20 Delphi 11 Alexandria | suffix 290 | packages/110  | LIBD29x32, LIBD29x64\par
\pard\sb0\sa60\li360\cf3\f1\fs20 Delphi 12 Athens     | suffix 360 | packages/12   | LIBD36x32, LIBD36x64\par
\pard\sb0\sa60\li360\cf3\f1\fs20 Delphi 13 Florence   | suffix 370 | packages/13   | LIBD37x32, LIBD37x64\par

\pard\sb240\sa120\cf2\f0\fs28\b Option A \emdash  Use Pre-compiled Binaries (Recommended for Beginners)\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 1.\tab Download or clone the repository from {\cf3\f1\fs20 https://github.com/K7LER/KingCal}\cf1\f0\fs22 .\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 2.\tab Open Delphi.\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 3.\tab Go to {\b Tools > Options > Language > Delphi > Library} and select your target platform (Win32 or Win64).\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 4.\tab Under {\b Library path}, click the {\b ...} button and add the path to the appropriate LIB folder.\par
\pard\fi-360\li1440\sb0\sa60\cf3\f1\fs20 Example for Delphi 13, 64-bit:  C:\KingCal\LIBD37x64\RELEASE\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 5.\tab Under {\b Browsing path}, add the path to the {\cf3\f1\fs20 source\cf1\f0\fs22 } folder.\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 6.\tab Click OK. The components now appear in the Tool Palette under {\b KingCalendar}.\par

\pard\sb240\sa120\cf2\f0\fs28\b Option B \emdash  Compile from Source\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 1.\tab Download or clone the repository.\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 2.\tab Open Delphi and configure the Library path and Browsing path as described in Option A (steps 3\endash 5).\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 3.\tab Open the group project for your Delphi version. Example for Delphi 13:\par
\pard\fi-360\li1440\sb0\sa60\cf3\f1\fs20 packages\13\D13All.groupproj\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 4.\tab In the Project Manager, right-click the {\b runtime package} (e.g. {\cf3\f1\fs20 KingCalendar370.dpk}\cf1\f0\fs22 ) and choose {\b Build}.\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 5.\tab Right-click the {\b design-time package} (e.g. {\cf3\f1\fs20 dclKingCalendar370.dpk}\cf1\f0\fs22 ) and choose {\b Build}.\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 6.\tab Right-click the design-time package again and choose {\b Install}. Delphi will confirm the components were registered.\par
\pard\fi-360\li720\sb0\sa80\cf1\f0\fs22 7.\tab To build for 64-bit, change the active platform in the Project Manager to Win64 and build the runtime package again.\par

\pard\sb0\sa160\cf1\f0\fs22 {\b Note:} The design-time package is always 32-bit (it runs inside the IDE itself). You only need to build the runtime package for each platform your application targets.\par

\pard\sb240\sa0\cf3\f0\fs16 ____________________________________________\par

